  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1106 AM MDT Fri Jul 3 2026\n\nElongated high across much of the southern US with the western\nedge near the AZ/NM border region. We have pushed some low level\nmoisture to near the Rio Grande and it will linger around through\nthe next week as east to southeast surface winds develop. This\nmoisture isn't what we would expect in the peak of the monsoon\nseason with dew points only in the 40s during the afternoon, but\nit will be enough for storms to develop over area mountains and\npush onto the lowlands with outflows each afternoon/evening. With\nthe holiday weekend here, I did play it safe and put in some low\nPoPs further south and west than the NBM had. Good agreement\nbetween the GFS and even the CAMs that tomorrow will see some\npretty strong outflows coming out of the Sacs and through the El\nPaso and surrounding areas between 6-9pm. This looks to be the \npattern through the next week as an upper high develops over the \nsouthern Rockies and then retrogrades a little bit later in the \nweek with the region coming under a more northeasterly flow. \n\nHigh temperatures will generally be in the upper 90s to around \n103 for the lowlands. Overnight lows will be trending up, \nespecially out west once the moisture moves in. Tonight still\nlooks to be below guidance with very dry air remaining in place,\nbut by Sun/Mon, the dew points rise from the teens/20s into the\n40s.",
